<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Lübeck</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1629">1629</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Peace of Lübeck · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> of 1629 ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-iv-of-denmark">Christian IV of Denmark</a>'s intervention in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. Defeated by the imperial armies of Tilly and Wallenstein, which had occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a>, Christian recovered his lands without paying an indemnity but promised to stay out of the affairs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lubeck.jpg" alt="Treaty of Lübeck" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wallenstein et al. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titelblatt_Luebecker_Frieden.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Lower Saxon or Emperor's War, the Danish phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, began in 1625. After early Danish successes under Christian IV and Ernst von Mansfeld, the tide turned in 1626 at the battles of Dessau Bridge and Lutter am Barenberge. Johann Tserclaes, Count of Tilly, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein">Albrecht von Wallenstein</a> joined their armies in the summer of 1627, invaded Holstein in September and advanced through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> as far as the Limfjord.</p><p>Christian still held the western Baltic and the Danish isles. In 1628 his fleet raided the occupied coasts and destroyed Wallenstein's naval facilities in Ålborg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wismar">Wismar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greifswald">Greifswald</a>, and in April he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, with whom he supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a>'s successful resistance. His defeat at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolgast">Wolgast</a> in August, the last battle of the war, left him ready to negotiate, and Wallenstein, alarmed by the Scandinavian alliance, was ready too.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>A proposal drafted by Tilly and Wallenstein in September 1627 would have had Christian buy back Holstein, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> for two million Reichstaler each; the Danish council of the realm accepted it as a basis, but Christian rejected it. Envoys met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> in January 1629, and the real bargain was struck in secret talks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a> in Mecklenburg. Concluded on 22 May 1629 and ratified by Emperor Ferdinand II on 7 June, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a> added as a party, the treaty restored Christian's pre-war possessions. He gave up his claims to the Lower Saxon bishoprics and his alliances with the North German states, and Tilly failed to make him pay the imperial war costs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The treaty marked the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from a major European power to a politically insignificant state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> took its place as the Nordic power, landing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> in 1630 and later, from the Torstenson War onwards, taking Denmark's provinces across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kattegat">Kattegat</a> and Norway's Jämtland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harjedalen">Härjedalen</a>. Ferdinand was disappointed with the terms Wallenstein had obtained, and the treaty deepened the rift between Christian and his council. Without Danish protection the North German states faced the Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">Restitution</a>, issued during the negotiations, which sought the return of church lands secularised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Lübeck” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
